        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Airless, HVLP and Handheld: How Each Type Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Airless sprayers</strong> use a pump to push paint at very high pressure, often up to around 3,000 psi on homeowner units, through a tiny tip. The pressure breaks the paint into a fine fan of droplets. Airless is the fastest way to apply paint to large areas and can handle thick latex without thinning. The cost is overspray: a fine mist that drifts and lands on everything nearby, which means more masking.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>HVLP sprayers</strong>, short for high volume, low pressure, push plenty of low-pressure air from a turbine across the paint stream, breaking it up more gently. The result is less overspray and better control, which is why HVLP is favored for cabinets, furniture and fine finishes. HVLP is slower than airless, and thicker paints usually need thinning to spray well.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Handheld airless sprayers</strong> combine an airless pump and paint cup in one handheld unit. They provide airless finish quality without hoses or buckets, and they are convenient for trim, doors and small rooms. Their limitation is capacity: small cups need frequent refilling, and they are not practical for whole-house exteriors.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Low-pressure airless</strong> units, sometimes called high-efficiency airless, sit between the two, spraying at lower pressure than conventional airless sprayers. Manufacturers say this reduces overspray while still handling unthinned latex in many cases. They are slower than full airless sprayers but more forgiving for beginners.
          </p>

