        <div id="match-job" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Matching the Sprayer to the Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>House exteriors and long fences.</strong> A stand-mounted airless sprayer such as the Graco Magnum X7 is the right tool. It draws from a 5-gallon bucket and covers siding, trim and fences quickly. For fences and decks, consider coatings designed for spraying; our guides to the <a href="/best-fence-stain/">best fence stain</a> and <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">best deck stain</a> cover suitable products.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Interior rooms.</strong> Spraying walls and ceilings is fastest in empty rooms such as new construction or before moving in. In furnished rooms, masking often takes longer than rolling. If you spray ceilings, choose a ceiling paint that sprays well; our guide to the <a href="/best-ceiling-paint/">best ceiling paint</a> covers options and rolling technique for when spraying is not practical.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cabinets, doors and furniture.</strong> HVLP or handheld airless sprayers give the smoothest finish with the most control. HVLP has the least overspray, making it easier to use in a garage or workshop, while handheld airless offers more speed. Cabinet projects benefit from a dedicated spray area with good ventilation.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Floors.</strong> Most floor paints and coatings are designed to be rolled, not sprayed. Rolling pushes the coating into the pores of the concrete or wood and gives better control over film thickness. If you are painting a garage floor or porch, see our guides to the <a href="/best-concrete-floor-paint/">best concrete floor paint</a> and <a href="/best-porch-paint/">best porch paint</a>, and use a sprayer for walls, railings and trim instead.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE MATCH-UP</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Big and flat: airless. Small and fine: HVLP. In between: handheld.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If most of your projects are exteriors, fences and empty rooms, choose airless. If most are cabinets and furniture, choose HVLP. If you mostly do trim, doors and touch-ups, a handheld airless unit is the most convenient choice.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="tips" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Airless Tips and Pressure, Decoded
          </h2>

          <p>
            Airless tips are labeled with three digits, such as 515 or 311. The first digit, doubled, gives the approximate fan width in inches when sprayed about 12 inches from the surface. The last two digits give the orifice size in thousandths of an inch. A 515 tip sprays a fan about 10 inches wide through a 0.015 inch orifice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Orifice size should match the material. Thin stains and lacquers use smaller orifices, around 0.009 to 0.013 inch. Interior latex paint commonly uses 0.013 to 0.017 inch. Thicker exterior paints and primers use 0.017 inch or larger. Check both the paint label and the sprayer's maximum tip size, because a sprayer cannot keep pressure with a tip larger than its pump supports.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fan width depends on the surface. Narrow fans, such as a 2xx or 3xx tip, are for trim, spindles and doors. Wider fans, such as 5xx or 6xx, are for walls and siding. Many homeowners own two tips: a fine-finish tip for trim and cabinets and a general tip for walls and exteriors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the lowest pressure that gives a clean pattern. Start low, spray a test pass on cardboard, and increase pressure until the pattern has no heavy lines, called tails, at the edges. Too much pressure increases overspray and wears the tip faster. Tips wear over time; a worn tip produces a narrower fan and a heavier center line, and should be replaced.
          </p>

