        <div id="thinning" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Thinning, Straining and Viscosity
          </h2>

          <p>
            Airless sprayers usually spray latex without thinning, but check the paint label. Some thick exterior paints and primers spray better with a small amount of thinning, and some sprayers specify limits. Never thin beyond the paint manufacturer's recommendation, as over-thinning reduces coverage and durability.
          </p>

          <p>
            HVLP sprayers usually need thinner material. Many come with a viscosity cup: dip it in paint, lift it, and time how long it takes to drain. The sprayer manual gives a target range for different materials. Thin latex with water and oil-based paints with the solvent the paint label specifies, adding small amounts at a time and retesting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strain all paint before spraying, even new paint. Small lumps, dried skin or debris can clog tips and filters. Use a disposable mesh strainer bag over a clean bucket. Many airless sprayers also have a filter in the gun and at the pump inlet; keeping them clean prevents spitting and uneven patterns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test before you spray the real surface. Spray a piece of cardboard and look for an even pattern without heavy edges, spitting or droplets. Adjust thinning, pressure or tip size as needed. A few minutes of testing saves time and prevents drips and runs on the project.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="masking-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Masking, Overspray and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Overspray travels farther than most people expect, especially outdoors. Cover everything you do not want painted: windows, doors, light fixtures, floors and furniture. Use plastic sheeting, drop cloths and masking paper, sealed with painter's tape. Outdoors, move cars well away and avoid spraying on windy days, as mist can drift onto vehicles, neighbors' property and plants.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wear protection. A respirator rated for the material, at minimum a properly fitted N95 for water-based paints and an organic vapor cartridge respirator for solvent-based coatings, protects your lungs from fine mist. Safety glasses or goggles, a hat and coveralls keep paint off your skin and out of your eyes. Ventilate indoor spaces with fans blowing out of windows.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">AIRLESS SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">An airless sprayer can inject paint through skin. Treat it like a power tool.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Never point the gun at yourself or anyone else, never put your hand in front of the tip, and always engage the trigger lock and relieve pressure before cleaning or changing tips. An injection injury may look minor but is a medical emergency; seek emergency care immediately and tell the doctor it was an injection injury.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Follow the manufacturer's pressure relief procedure every time you stop spraying, change a tip, or clean the gun. Keep the tip guard in place, as it helps prevent accidental contact with the high-pressure stream. Do not spray solvent-based materials near open flames, pilot lights or sparks, and keep the work area ventilated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before spraying near windows and doors, check that caulking and trim are in good condition; spraying will not fill gaps. Our guide to the <a href="/best-exterior-caulk/">best exterior caulk</a> covers products for sealing gaps before painting siding and trim.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Spray Technique for an Even Finish
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hold the gun perpendicular to the surface and keep it about 10 to 12 inches away for airless, or closer for HVLP, as the manual recommends. Move your whole arm, not just your wrist. Arcing the gun by flexing the wrist leaves the ends of each pass thinner than the middle, creating uneven coverage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start moving before pulling the trigger and release the trigger before stopping. This prevents heavy spots at the beginning and end of each pass. Overlap each pass by about half the fan width so the coating builds evenly. Maintain a steady speed; moving too slowly causes runs, while moving too quickly leaves thin areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spray edges and corners first, then fill in larger areas. On walls, spray in horizontal or vertical passes consistently. On trim and doors, follow the shape of the piece. For cabinets and furniture, spray the less visible areas first, then the most visible surfaces last for the best finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            On interior walls and ceilings, many painters back-roll after spraying: they spray a section and immediately roll it lightly with a roller. Back-rolling improves adhesion and evens out texture, especially on porous surfaces and new drywall. Exteriors on rough siding and masonry also benefit from back-rolling the first coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleanup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Cleanup and Storage: Where Sprayers Live or Die
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean the sprayer immediately after use. Latex dries quickly in tips, filters and hoses, and dried paint is the main cause of sprayer problems. For water-based paints, run warm water through the system until the output is no longer cloudy; for oil-based materials, flush with the solvent the coating manufacturer specifies, disposing of it according to local rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            Disassemble and clean the parts that collect paint: the tip, tip guard, gun filter and inlet filter. Soak them in clean water or solvent and brush them out with a soft brush. Do not use metal tools on the tip orifice, as scratches change the spray pattern.
          </p>

          <p>
            For airless sprayers, follow the manufacturer's storage instructions, which often include filling the pump with a storage fluid or pump conditioner to prevent corrosion and keep seals from drying out. Store sprayers where they will not freeze, as water left in the pump can freeze and damage it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect cleanup to take 20 to 45 minutes for a full airless sprayer and less for handheld units and HVLP. That time is part of the decision: for a single small project, cleanup can make spraying slower than brushing or rolling. For large projects, the time saved spraying far exceeds the cleanup.
          </p>

