        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is a Paint Sprayer Worth Buying?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sprayer pays off on large, open or detailed projects: house exteriors, long fences, decks with many balusters, empty rooms, and cabinets or furniture where a smooth finish matters. On these jobs, a sprayer can apply paint several times faster than brushing or rolling, and often gives a better finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            A sprayer is usually not worth it for a single furnished room, a small touch-up or a project with a lot of masking for little painting. In those cases, the time spent masking and cleaning outweighs the time saved. A good roller and brush remain the best tools for many interior repaints.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, budget HVLP sprayers cost roughly $60 to $150, handheld airless and mid-range HVLP units roughly $150 to $350, and homeowner stand-mounted airless sprayers roughly $300 to $600. Cordless pro handheld units cost more. Renting an airless sprayer is an option for a single large project and typically costs roughly $70 to $120 per day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Paint use also increases with spraying. Sprayers typically use more paint than rolling because of overspray, often an extra 20 to 30 percent or more outdoors. Factor that into the cost, along with tips, filters, masking supplies and cleaning materials. For people with recurring projects, the time savings usually justify the purchase.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying an airless sprayer for small furniture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Airless sprayers are fast but produce lots of overspray and require more cleanup. For a dresser, a few chairs or a small set of cabinets, an HVLP or handheld sprayer is easier to control and clean. Match the sprayer to the size of the project rather than buying the most powerful option.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the strainer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even fresh paint can contain lumps or debris that clog tips and filters, causing spitting and uneven patterns. Straining every bucket through a mesh strainer bag takes a minute and prevents interruptions. Clean the gun and inlet filters regularly so paint flows smoothly throughout the project.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the wrong tip size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tip too small for the material produces a weak, uneven pattern; a tip too large wastes paint, creates runs and can exceed the sprayer's capacity. Check the paint label and the sprayer's maximum tip size, and keep a fine-finish tip for trim and a larger tip for walls and exteriors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flicking the wrist at the end of each pass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flexing the wrist changes the distance between the gun and surface during each pass, creating thick centers and thin edges. Keep the gun perpendicular and move your whole arm in a straight line. Start moving before pulling the trigger and release it before you stop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving paint in the sprayer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Latex dries quickly in tips, filters and hoses. Leaving the sprayer uncleaned, even for an hour or two, can clog it and damage seals. Clean immediately after use, follow the storage instructions, and store the sprayer where it will not freeze.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

