        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which type of paint sprayer suits a first-time buyer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the projects. For house exteriors, fences and large rooms, an airless sprayer like the Graco Magnum X7 is the most useful. For cabinets and furniture, an HVLP sprayer gives more control and less overspray. For trim, doors and small rooms, a handheld airless sprayer offers convenience without hoses or buckets.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does paint need thinning before it goes through a sprayer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Airless sprayers usually spray latex without thinning, though some thick paints benefit from a small amount. HVLP sprayers often need thinner material, so use the viscosity cup and the sprayer manual's guidance. Never thin beyond the paint manufacturer's recommendation, and always strain paint before spraying to prevent clogs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much more paint does spraying use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Spraying usually uses more paint than rolling because of overspray, often an extra 20 to 30 percent or more outdoors, depending on wind, technique and sprayer type. HVLP sprayers generally waste less than airless sprayers. Using the correct tip size and lowest effective pressure helps reduce waste.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a paint sprayer handle a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Airless sprayers and some handheld airless sprayers can spray ceilings efficiently, especially in empty rooms or new construction. Use a ceiling paint suitable for spraying, mask walls and floors thoroughly, and wear eye protection. Many painters back-roll after spraying for a more uniform finish on drywall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are airless paint sprayers dangerous?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They can be. The high-pressure stream can inject paint through skin, which is a medical emergency even if the wound looks small. Never point the gun at anyone, keep the tip guard in place, use the trigger lock, and relieve pressure before cleaning or changing tips. Wear eye protection and a respirator suited to the material.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does cleaning a sprayer take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expect roughly 20 to 45 minutes for a full airless sprayer and less for handheld and HVLP units. Clean immediately after use, flushing with water for latex or the specified solvent for oil-based materials, and clean the tip and filters. Follow the manufacturer's storage instructions to keep the pump and seals in good condition.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I rent or buy a paint sprayer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rent if you have a single large project, such as a house exterior, and do not expect to spray again soon. Buy if you have recurring projects such as fences, decks, rooms and cabinets, or if you want a smaller HVLP or handheld unit for detail work. Rental airless units are often larger and more powerful than homeowner models.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

